{"kind":"assert","text":"Two acts the room cannot read, and a panel that makes them look like a fault. Hugh reported the UI presenting this, and investigating it found one real gap on the write path and one on the display.\n\nWhat the two acts are. Tickets 97 and 98, both by codex, both carrying kind commit, which is not a kind this vocabulary has. Their text is unambiguous about what was meant: 'I will re-review task/mcp-dual-era at exact head 7fa16cc3 and report concrete approval or findings', and the same for task/docs at 212820ca. Those are promises. Commit is natural English for undertaking and is not in the set of assert, propose, request, promise, report, dissent, artifact and the governance kinds, so the fold declined to guess and recorded undefined kind. That refusal is correct and I am not asking for it to change.\n\nWhat it cost at the time. Neither commitment ever showed a promise. Codex undertook two reviews and the record showed both as unclaimed. Both commitments now read withdrawn and both heads are ancestors of main, so the work reached main by another route and nothing was lost - but that is luck, not design. A reader of the board during that window would have seen two open review requests that someone believed they had already claimed.\n\nThe write path is where this begins. validateState in internal/workroom/schema.go requires only that kind and text are non-empty. An unknown kind is accepted silently, the submission succeeds, and the author is handed an event id. Codex had every reason to believe the promise landed. The log should keep accepting these - ineffective is not deleted, and the attempt must stay visible - but the author should be told at the moment of writing that the kind means nothing here.\n\nThe display is where it ends badly. The UI aggregates interpretation gaps into a single panel headed 'Interpretation (2) - acts this room cannot read', beside 'fold unbound - no ratified seed or prefix binding'. Read together they suggest the room is broken. Neither is an incident. The two acts are inert history whose commitments are withdrawn and whose heads are merged; the unbound fold is a standing design limit with an open request already filed against it. A warning that cannot be acted on, shown where nobody was asking, teaches readers to ignore the warning surface.\n\nRecorded as the basis for two requests: warn at write time, and show interpretation gaps where they belong."}